{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 26, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct", "content": "i recently watched \"the social network\" and i must say it's a film that left me both impressed and frustrated. directed by david fincher, this movie tells the story of the founding of facebook and its impact on the lives of the people involved.\n\nthe film starts with the story of mark zuckerberg, played by jesse eisenberg, who is portrayed as a socially awkward high school student who creates a website called \"face mash\" with his college roommates and future business partners, edgar and dwight. the initial idea of the site is to create a website that would allow harvard students to connect with each other, but it eventually evolves into something much bigger.\n\nas the story unfolds, we see the formation of facebook and its rapid rise to popularity. the film also explores the personal relationships between the characters, particularly the complex and tumultuous friendship between mark and edgar. the chemistry between eisenberg and armie hammer, who plays the role of sean parker, is undeniable, and their on-screen dynamic is electric.\n\none of the standout aspects of the film is its cinematography. david fincher's signature dark and moody style is on full display, with the use of shadows, lighting, and camera angles to create a sense of tension and unease. the score, composed by t Trent reznor and atticus ross, is equally impressive, perfectly capturing the mood and atmosphere of each scene.\n\nthe performances in the film are also noteworthy, with jesse eisenberg delivering a nuanced and complex portrayal of mark zuckerberg. armie hammer, as sean parker, brings a sense of charisma and charm to the role, while david fincher's cameo as nelson peltz is a great touch.\n\nhowever, the film is not without its flaws. some of the characters, particularly the women in the story, are underdeveloped and feel like they are being used as plot devices rather 